BspTree: bound a leaf node's polygon range before it indexes the polygon list.

`BspTree::load` collects each leaf's polygons straight out of the index list:

	for (auto idx : this->leaf_node_indices) {
		auto& node = this->nodes[idx];

		for (uint32_t i = 0; i < node.polygon_count; ++i) {
			this->leaf_polygons.push_back(this->polygon_indices[node.polygon_index + i]);
		}
	}

`polygon_index` and `polygon_count` are two `uint32`s read off the node in the
TREE chunk (`0xC040`), while `polygon_indices` is sized and filled by a
*different* chunk (POLYGONS, `0xC010`). Nothing in the format ties the two
together, and nothing here checks them against each other, so a node whose
range runs past the end of the list is an unchecked `std::vector::operator[]`
read past the allocation — and the list is empty entirely if the POLYGONS
chunk never parsed, which a single corrupted chunk id is enough to arrange.

Found by fuzzing `test/fixtures/minimal.g2.zen`: 40 seeds of 20 random byte
writes confined to the archive's entry stream produced four `0xC0000005`s and
one hang, and seed 2 delta-debugged down to **one byte** — file offset 1230,
the high byte of the `0xC010` chunk's id. Flipping it hides the polygon list
from the chunk dispatcher while the tree still names two polygons in it. The
committed regression test seeds the same defect from the other end and by
structure, writing an out-of-range `polygonIndex` into the one leaf node, so it
does not depend on the fixture's chunk order.

The fix is the same narrow shape as `0027` and `0029`: check the count the file
supplies against what it is actually allowed to address, and throw a named
`ParserError` instead of trusting it. `polygon_count` is widened to 64 bits for
the comparison so the sum cannot wrap. This is one more instance of the
unvalidated-count class in `docs/plans/level-editor.md` §16.11, not the
class-level fix — `_parse_bsp_nodes` still recurses once per file-supplied flag
bit with no depth bound, `BspTree::load`'s OUTDOORS branch resizes from
unchecked counts, and the general fix (making `ReadMemory::seek` clamp or
throw) is still deferred.

Upstreamable as-is: a plain bounds-check bug, no API change, one guard.

